### What are the five BlockingQueue implementations?

Java provides five standard BlockingQueue implementations, each designed for a specific concurrency use case.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class BlockingQueueDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        // ArrayBlockingQueue: fixed capacity, one lock, optional fairness
        BlockingQueue<String> arrayBQ = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(100, true);

        // LinkedBlockingQueue: two locks, higher throughput
        BlockingQueue<String> linkedBQ = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(1000);

        // PriorityBlockingQueue: heap-ordered, unbounded
        BlockingQueue<String> priorityBQ = new PriorityBlockingQueue<>();

        // SynchronousQueue: zero capacity, direct handoff
        BlockingQueue<String> syncQ = new SynchronousQueue<>();

        // DelayQueue: time-based release
        DelayQueue<DelayedTask> delayQ = new DelayQueue<>();
    }
}
```

### How are they different?

| Queue | Capacity | Locks | Order | Blocks on | Use case |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| ArrayBlockingQueue | Fixed | 1 | FIFO | put + take | Bounded buffer, fairness required |
| LinkedBlockingQueue | Bounded/unbounded | 2 | FIFO | put + take | Producer-consumer, ThreadPool default |
| PriorityBlockingQueue | Unbounded | 1 | Priority | take only | Priority processing |
| SynchronousQueue | 0 | N/A | Handoff | put + take | Direct handoff, CachedPool |
| DelayQueue | Unbounded | 1 | Delay | take only | Scheduled tasks, retry |

### Why does LinkedBlockingQueue have higher throughput?

LinkedBlockingQueue uses two separate locks: a `putLock` for producers and a `takeLock` for consumers. This means put() and take() can run concurrently on different threads without contending for the same lock. ArrayBlockingQueue uses a single ReentrantLock for both operations, so producers and consumers serialize on the same lock.

```java
// LinkedBlockingQueue internal structure (simplified):
// putLock -> protects the tail of the linked list
// takeLock -> protects the head of the linked list
// Producer adds at tail (putLock), Consumer removes from head (takeLock)
// Both can proceed simultaneously!

// ArrayBlockingQueue internal structure (simplified):
// Single lock -> protects the entire array
// Producer and consumer must take turns
```

### When would you use each one in a banking system?

At National Bank of India, different queues serve different purposes.

```java
package com.bank.queues;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class BankingQueueExamples {

    // Settlement queue: bounded, high-throughput producer-consumer
    private final BlockingQueue<Settlement> settlementQueue =
        new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(10_000);

    // VIP trade queue: highest-value trades processed first
    private final BlockingQueue<Trade> vipQueue =
        new PriorityBlockingQueue<>(100,
            (a, b) -> b.getValue().compareTo(a.getValue())); // highest value first

    // Retry queue: failed payments retried after a 30-second delay
    private final DelayQueue<RetryablePayment> retryQueue = new DelayQueue<>();
}
```

### How does SynchronousQueue work as direct handoff?

SynchronousQueue has zero internal capacity. `put()` blocks until a consumer calls `take()`, and `take()` blocks until a producer calls `put()`. This provides direct handoff with no buffering. It is used internally by `Executors.newCachedThreadPool()` because the pool needs a new thread for every task that arrives when no idle thread is available.

### How does DelayQueue enforce time-based release?

Elements must implement the `Delayed` interface. `take()` blocks until the element's delay has expired. Elements are ordered by expiry time.

```java
package com.bank.retry;

import java.util.concurrent.Delayed;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class RetryablePayment implements Delayed {
    private final String paymentId;
    private final long retryAt;  // epoch millis

    public RetryablePayment(String paymentId, long delayMs) {
        this.paymentId = paymentId;
        this.retryAt = System.currentTimeMillis() + delayMs;
    }

    @Override
    public long getDelay(TimeUnit unit) {
        return unit.convert(retryAt - System.currentTimeMillis(), TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
    }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Delayed other) {
        return Long.compare(this.retryAt, ((RetryablePayment) other).retryAt);
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

Using an unbounded LinkedBlockingQueue (no capacity specified) with a ThreadPoolExecutor hides pool saturation. The queue grows without limit, consuming memory, and tasks wait indefinitely instead of being rejected. Always specify a capacity.

```java
// WRONG: unbounded queue hides saturation
new LinkedBlockingQueue<>()  // grows to Integer.MAX_VALUE

// CORRECT: bounded queue with rejection policy
new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(1000)
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Is PriorityBlockingQueue thread-safe? Yes, but its iterator does NOT guarantee any particular order. Only poll() and take() guarantee priority order.
- Can SynchronousQueue hold even one element? No. It has zero capacity. Every put() must rendezvous with a take().
- What queue does ThreadPoolExecutor use by default? It depends on how you create it. Executors.newFixedThreadPool uses unbounded LinkedBlockingQueue. Executors.newCachedThreadPool uses SynchronousQueue.
